<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François-Timoléon de Choisy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1724">1724</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Abbé de Choisy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François-Timoléon de Choisy was a French abbé, writer and member of the Académie Française, known for his memoirs, historical and religious writings and travel accounts. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to a family attached to the court, he was made an abbé in childhood. In 1676 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with the cardinal de Bouillon, and in 1685 he accompanied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chevalier-de-chaumont">Chevalier de Chaumont</a> on a diplomatic mission to the Kingdom of Siam; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> later owned his account of Siam. He entered the Académie in 1687 and wrote an eleven-volume history of the Church. His gossiping Mémoires of 1727 and his autobiographical writings, which describe his wearing of women's clothing, are of questioned reliability.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-timoleon-de-choisy.jpg" alt="François-Timoléon de Choisy" width="330" height="597" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbé_de_Choisy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Choisy was born on 16 August 1644. His father served in the household of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, and his mother, who was on close terms with Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was often invited to amuse the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. According to his own memoirs, his mother dressed him in girls' clothing until he was eighteen; he briefly wore men's clothes and then returned to women's dress, reportedly on the advice of Madame de La Fayette.</p><p>An inheritance after his mother's death allowed him to live without regular employment. He wrote that he wore elaborate women's clothing until he was publicly criticised by the duc de Montausier, after which he withdrew to the provinces. The historian Paul Scott has pointed to implausibilities, contradictions and anachronisms in these accounts, and to the lack of any contemporary corroboration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Money troubles later led him to live at his benefice of Sainte-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, where he came to know Bussy-Rabutin. After his journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1676 he wrote that a serious illness had led to a religious conversion. With the Abbé Dangeau he wrote the Quatre dialogues sur l'immortalité de l'âme (1684), which explained their conversion. He was admitted to the Académie Française on 24 July 1687, and his Histoire de l'Eglise appeared between 1703 and 1723.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Choisy died on 2 October 1724. His Mémoires, with their detailed portraits of his contemporaries, passed through many editions and were edited in 1868 by M. de Lescure. Some of his letters survive in the correspondence of Bussy-Rabutin. He is said to have burnt some of his more indiscreet writings, but he left many unpublished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><p>Part of this material was used without acknowledgement in an anonymous Histoire de madame la comtesse de Barres (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, 1735) and, heavily edited, in the Vie de M. l'abbé de Choisy (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, 1742), which Paul Lacroix attributed to Lenglet Dufresnoy. Lacroix finally edited the text in 1870 as Aventures de l'abbé de Choisy. His autobiographical writings, describing his frequent use of women's clothing in private and public life, have been a subject of scholarly debate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François-Timoléon de Choisy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>François-Timoléon de Choisy</b> wrote in 1685 that “the king has absolute power”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1696" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of François-Timoléon de Choisy"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1696">What was here in 1696? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
